# Regarding weakness 2

Thank you for bringing up the concern about the value of $p$.

**With respect to Adahessian**, since it takes the square root of the “square” of the Hessian in its implementation, we hold the belief that the correspondence $p = -1$ is more suitable. 

**Concerning Adam**, you are indeed correct, and $p = -1/2$ would have been a more appropriate choice. We have taken this matter into consideration and highlighted it by revising the paper.

**Regarding SAM**, it can be associated with p=1 through the following simple derivation.

Recall the gradient computation in SAM, denoted as $\boldsymbol{g}_{\text{SAM}}$, which involves computing the gradient at a perturbed parameter location to maximize the loss locally:

\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{g}_{\text{SAM}} = \nabla\mathcal{L}\left(\boldsymbol{\theta} + \frac{\rho}{||\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})||}\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\right),
\end{equation}

where $\rho$ is the neighborhood size. Let us define the perturbation vector $\boldsymbol{\delta}$ as:

\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\delta} = \frac{\rho}{||\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})||}\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta}).
\end{equation}

Assuming that $\rho$ is sufficiently small, we can apply a first-order Taylor expansion of the gradient $\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta} + \boldsymbol{\delta})$ around $\boldsymbol{\theta}$. Let $\boldsymbol{H} = \nabla^2\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})$ denote the Hessian matrix of the loss function at $\boldsymbol{\theta}$. The approximation proceeds as follows:

\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{g}_{\text{SAM}} &\approx \nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \boldsymbol{H} \boldsymbol{\delta} \nonumber = \nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \boldsymbol{H} \left( \frac{\rho}{||\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})||}\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) \right) \nonumber = \nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \frac{\rho}{||\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})||} \boldsymbol{H} \nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta}).
\end{align}

By factoring out the gradient term $\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})$, we obtain the final approximate form:

\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{g}_{\text{SAM}} \approx \left( I + \frac{\rho}{||\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})||} \boldsymbol{H} \right) \nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta}).
\end{equation}

This derivation illustrates that the effective gradient used in SAM is approximately equivalent to the standard gradient preconditioned by the matrix $\left( I + \frac{\rho}{||\nabla\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})||} \boldsymbol{H} \right)$. Therefore, SAM corresponds to $p = 1$ in our formulation.

We have also included this derivation in the appendix.
